(14) members of the committee who are not members of a subcommittee may participate in the proceedings of that subcommittee but shall not vote, move any motion or be counted for the purpose of a quorum; 

(15) the committee or any subcommittee have power to: 

(a) call for witnesses to attend and for documents to be produced; 

(b) conduct proceedings at any place it sees fit; 

(c) sit in public or in private; 

(d) report from time to time; 

(e) adjourn from time to time and to sit during any adjournment of the Senate and the House of Representatives; and 

(f) conduct meetings for the purpose of private briefings at any time; 

(16) the committee or any subcommittee have power to consider and make use of the evidence and records of the Joint Committees on Foreign Affairs and Defence, and Foreign Affairs, Defence and Trade, appointed during previous Parliaments; 

(17) the provisions of this resolution, so far as they are inconsistent with the standing orders, have effect notwithstanding anything contained in the standing orders; and 

(2) a message be sent to the Senate acquainting it of this resolution and requesting that it concur and take action accordingly.

Presentation 

The following notices were given: 

Mr Pasin to move: 

That this House: 

(1) notes a leaked Australia Post business case which outlines plans to close metropolitan licensed Post Offices and pursue widespread 'format conversions' across regional and rural Australia; 

(2) acknowledges that: 

(a) Australians should not have to learn about significant changes to one of our most trusted public institutions through leaked documents; 

(b) rising postage costs and Australia Post's declining service standards are already having a negative impact on regional Australia; 

(c) local post offices in regional communities are far more than places to send parcels and collect mail; 

(d) there are serious concerns with Australia Post's plans to reduce services and alter existing arrangements for licensed Post Offices; and 

(e) local rural and regional post offices should be protected; and 

(3) welcomes the Opposition-led Senate inquiry into Australia Post's secret plans to reduce services and change the future of licensed Post Offices. 

Mr Hamilton to move: 

That this House: 

(1) notes that the Commonwealth's Capacity Investment Scheme (CIS) represents one of the largest government interventions in Australia's electricity market, involving long-term contractual commitments and significant contingent financial liabilities on behalf of Australian taxpayers; 

(2) recognises that Parliament and the Australian public are entitled to clear, timely and meaningful information regarding: 

(a) the Commonwealth's current and projected financial exposure under the scheme; 

(b) the criteria used to assess and select successful projects; 

(c) whether projects represent value for money; 

(d) the extent to which supported projects would have proceeded without Commonwealth underwriting; and 

(e) whether the scheme is delivering measurable improvements in electricity reliability and affordability; 

(3) further notes with concern that, despite the substantial scale of public financial commitments under the scheme: 

(a) no dedicated performance audit has been undertaken by the Australian National Audit Office (ANAO); 

(b) limited information has been publicly released regarding project assessment methodologies, value-for-money analyses and taxpayer risk; and 

(c) Parliament has limited ability to independently assess whether the scheme is achieving its stated objectives or whether public resources are being used efficiently, effectively, economically and ethically; 

(4) acknowledges the ANAO's longstanding expectation that significant Commonwealth programs should be supported by transparent governance, meaningful performance measures and reporting that enables Parliament to assess whether public resources are being used appropriately; and 

(5) calls on the Government to: 

(a) publish the assessment framework, evaluation criteria and value-for-money methodology used for CIS tenders; 

(b) publish annual estimates of the Commonwealth's contingent liabilities and expected fiscal exposure under the scheme; 

(c) publicly report against measurable indicators of electricity reliability, consumer price impacts, investment additionality and emissions outcomes; 

(d) commission an independent evaluation of the effectiveness and efficiency of the CIS; and 

(e) request the Auditor-General to undertake a performance audit of the scheme, including its governance, procurement processes, management of taxpayer risk, performance measurement and achievement of intended policy outcomes. 

Mr Pasin to move: 

That this House: 

(1) notes that in April 2022, Australia introduced 35 per cent tariff on Russian and Belarusian timber imported into Australia in response to the war in Ukraine; 

(2) acknowledges that, in a process where products are relabelled in third world countries, Russian timber has been able to be rerouted through these countries to avoid direct tariff and sanctions, while allowing funds to flow back to Russia; and 

(3) urges the Government to close the loophole for all timber products containing Russian materials being rerouted into Australia. 

Mr Pasin to move: 

That this House: 

(1) welcomes the announcement that the WIN Network and Network 10 have reached a temporary three-month agreement to continue broadcasting Network 10 services across the Riverland and Limestone Coast in South Australia and in Griffith in New South Wales; 

(2) acknowledges that: 

(a) this agreement is a pause, not a solution; 

(b) regional Australians deserve the same access to free-to-air television as those living in our capital cities; 

(c) the challenges facing regional broadcasters extend well beyond this single commercial agreement; 

(d) broadcasters have been warning this Government for some time that the long-term future of regional free-to-air television is at risk; 

(e) the structure that broadcasters work within is outdated and does not reflect the current position of the industry; and 

(f) entire regional communities could eventually be forced to rely on satellite television instead of locally broadcast services; and 

(3) urges the Government to work with the industry on meaningful reforms that secure the future of regional broadcasting, before the three-month extension expires. 

